  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "For an **upper-upscale** hotel project in the Caribbean and Latin America (CALA), owners and developers typically shortlist brands based on destination type (urban vs. resort/all-inclusive), target market (U.S./Canadian feeder markets vs. intra-regional South American business/leisure), and conversion flexibility.\n\nThe upper-upscale segment sits just below full-fledged luxury (e.g., Four Seasons, St. Regis, Rosewood) and above upscale brands (e.g., Courtyard, AC Hotels, Novotel).\n\nBelow is a breakdown of the brands most commonly shortlisted by developers in CALA, categorized by parent company and strategic model.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Global Major Operators (Hard Brands)\n\nThese flagship full-service brands provide massive global reservation systems and strong loyalty program penetration (crucial for capturing high-yield North American travelers).\n\n*   **Marriott International**\n    *   **Westin Hotels & Resorts:** A leading brand in CALA for both urban centers and beach resorts/all-inclusive developments. Highly favored for wellness-oriented positioning.\n    *   **Marriott Hotels & Resorts:** The flagship full-service option, popular in primary urban markets (e.g., Mexico City, São Paulo, Bogotá) and large-scale resort developments.\n    *   **Renaissance Hotels:** Shortlisted for lifestyle-centric urban projects and select coastal destinations.\n*   **Hilton Worldwide**\n    *   **Hilton Hotels & Resorts:** A staple in primary Latin American capital cities and core Caribbean resort markets (e.g., Puerto Rico, Dominican Republic).\n    *   **Embassy Suites by Hilton:** Highly shortlisted for resort locations with space for suite-style layouts and family leisure travel.\n    *   **Canopy by Hilton:** Frequently considered for vibrant urban lifestyle developments.\n*   **Hyatt Hotels Corporation**\n    *   **Grand Hyatt:** Commonly chosen for large-scale urban or resort projects requiring extensive MICE (Meetings, Incentives, Conferences, Exhibitions) facilities.\n    *   **Hyatt Centric:** A top contender for urban lifestyle and leisure-adjacent developments catering to millennial/experiential travelers.\n*   **IHG Hotels & Resorts**\n    *   **Kimpton Hotels & Restaurants:** Rapidly growing in CALA (e.g., Mexico, Caribbean, Central America) for high-end boutique and lifestyle projects.\n    *   **Hotel Indigo:** Shortlisted for boutique projects in culturally rich urban hubs or emerging leisure destinations.\n*   **Accor**\n    *   **Pullman / Swissôtel / Mövenpick:** Widely shortlisted in South America (particularly Brazil, Colombia, Peru, and Chile) where Accor holds strong regional brand awareness and operational infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Soft Brand Collections (Extremely Popular in CALA)\n\nBecause many CALA projects involve converting independent boutique hotels, historic properties, or unique eco-resorts, **soft brands** are often at the top of developers' shortlists. They allow owners to keep unique design identities while leveraging global distribution platforms.\n\n*   **Autograph Collection (Marriott):** One of the most sought-after soft brands in the region for high-end boutique and resort projects.\n*   **Curio Collection by Hilton:** A major competitor to Autograph, seeing aggressive expansion in resort and island destinations.\n*   **The Unbound Collection by Hyatt:** Shortlisted for distinctive, historic, or high-concept luxury/upper-upscale properties.\n*   **Tribute Portfolio (Marriott) / Tapestry Collection (Hilton):** Shortlisted for upper-upscale projects with a strong local, community-focused lifestyle angle.\n*   **Vignette Collection (IHG):** An increasingly popular shortlist option for independent conversions in Central and South America.\n\n---\n\n### 3. All-Inclusive & Regional Specialists\n\nIn the Caribbean, Riviera Maya, Dominican Republic, and Central America, upper-upscale resort development is heavily dominated by **All-Inclusive (AI)** formats. \n\n*   **Hyatt Inclusive Collection:**\n    *   **Dreams Resorts & Spas:** Highly coveted for upper-upscale family-friendly all-inclusive developments.\n    *   **Breathless Resorts & Spas / Hyatt Vivid:** Popular shortlists for adult-oriented lifestyle all-inclusive concepts.\n*   **Hard Rock Hotels:** A dominant independent player in CALA, frequently shortlisted for entertainment-heavy, large-scale all-inclusive resorts and urban lifestyle assets.\n*   **Grupo Posadas (Mexico/LATAM):**\n    *   **Fiesta Americana / Fiesta Americana Grand:** Highly regarded in Mexico and Central America for regional business and resort distribution.\n*   **Meliá Hotels International:**\n    *   **Meliá Hotels & Resorts / Paradisus:** A major Spanish operator frequently shortlisted for resort developments across the Dominican Republic, Mexico, and South America.\n*   **Palladium Hotel Group:**\n    *   **Grand Palladium / TRS Hotels:** Widely considered for large beachfront all-inclusive master developments.\n\n---\n\n### Key Factors Developers Use to Finalize the Shortlist\n\n1.  **Feeder Markets & Loyalty Programs:** Over 60–70% of upper-upscale resort demand in the Caribbean and Mexico originates from North America. Brands tied to **Marriott Bonvoy, Hilton Honors, or World of Hyatt** carry a significant premium due to direct booking power.\n2.  **Conversion vs. New Build Flexibility:** Soft brands (Autograph, Curio) typically require lower PIP (Property Improvement Plan) capital expenditures compared to strict hard brands (Westin, Grand Hyatt).\n3.  **Branded Residential Integration:** Developers frequently look for brands that support branded residences (condos/villas) to help fund initial project financing.\n4.  **All-Inclusive Operator Expertise:** Operating an all-inclusive requires entirely different food, beverage, and cost-control systems. Developers pitching AI projects usually favor brands with dedicated regional AI management infrastructure (e.g., Hyatt's Inclusive Collection, Marriott All-Inclusive).",
